===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|puff}}, {{m|enm|puf}}, from {{inh|en|ang|pyf|t=a blast of wind, puff}}, {{onomatopoeic|en|title=imitative}}. Cognate with {{cog|gml|puf}}, {{m|gml|pof}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} A sharp [[exhalation]] of a small amount of [[breath]] through the mouth.
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The ability to [[breathe]] easily while [[exert]]ing oneself.
#: {{syn|en|wind}}
#: {{ux|en|out of '''puff'''}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A small quantity of [[gas]] or [[smoke]] in the air.
#: {{ux|en|'''puff''' of smoke}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A sudden but small [[gust]] of wind, smoke, etc.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1674|author=w:Thomas Flatman|title=Poems and Songs
|passage=to every '''puff''' of wind a slave}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=184|passage="The wind we had was only an early '''puff'''! You'll see we shall have our full allowance!"}}
# {{lb|en|informal|countable}} An act of [[inhale|inhaling]] smoke from a [[cigarette]], [[cigar]] or [[pipe]].
#: {{syn|en|drag}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1978
|author=w:Richard Nixon
|chapter=The Presidency 1969-1972
|title=RN: the Memoirs of Richard Nixon
|url=https://archive.org/details/rnmemoirsofricha00nixo/
|publisher=Grosset & Dunlap
|ISBN=0-448-14374-7
|LCCN=77-87793
|OCLC=760525066
|OL=7561812M
|page=572
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/rnmemoirsofricha00nixo/page/572/
|text=When I went to bed that night I found that I could not get to sleep. At five o'clock I got up and took a hot bath. I climbed back into bed and lighted one of the Chinese-made "Great Wall" cigars my hosts had thoughtfully provided, and sat '''puffing''' on the cigar and making notes about the events of the momentous week.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|slang}} The [[drug]] [[cannabis]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:marijuana}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[flamboyant]] or alluring [[statement]] of [[praise]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1902|author=Robert Marshall Grade|title=The Haunted Major
|passage={{...}} though I care not one straw for the personal '''puffs''' of which I myself am so often the subject {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1931|author=Bernard Shaw|title=Our Theatre in the Nineties|volume=24|page=246
|passage={{...}} we critics were not his fellow-guests, but simply [[deadhead]]s whose business it was to "[[dress the house]]" and write '''puffs'''.}}
# A portion of [[fabric]] gathered up so as to be left full in the middle.
#: {{ux|en|a sleeve with a '''puff''' at the shoulder}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A light [[cake]] filled with cream, cream cheese, etc.
#: {{syn|en|pastry|cream puff}}
#: {{ux|en|cream '''puff'''}}
# A [[puffball]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1598|author=w:John Florio|title=A Worlde of Wordes, or Most Copious, and Exact Dictionarie in Italian and English|location=London|publisher=Edward Blount|page=47|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A00991.0001.001
|passage=''Bozzacchio'', an acorne. Also a '''puffe''' or [[mushrump]] full of dust.}}
# A [[powder puff]].
# {{lb|en|dated|slang}} A [[puffer]], one who is employed by the owner or seller of goods sold at auction to bid up the price; an act or [[scam]] of that type.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1842|chapter=A Paper on Puffing|title=Ainsworth's Magazine
|passage=Is nothing to be said in praise of the "Emporiums" and "Repositories" and "Divans," which formerly were mere insignificant tailors', toymen's, and tobacconists' shops? Is the transition from the barber's pole to the revolving bust of the perruquier, nothing? — the leap from the bare counter-traversed shop to the carpeted and mirrored saloon of trade, nothing? Are they not, one and all, practical '''puffs''', intended to invest commerce with elegance, and to throw a halo round extravagance?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1848|author=Mrs. White|chapter=Puffs and Puffing|title=Sharpe's London Magazine
|passage=Here the duke is made the vehicle of the tailor's advertisement, and the prelusive compliments, ostensibly meant for his grace, merge into a covert recommendation of the coat. Several specimens might be given of this species of '''puff''', which is to be met with in almost every paper, and is a favourite form with booksellers, professional men, &c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2008|author=David Paton-Williamspage|title=Katterfelto|page=xii
|passage=He was the eighteenth century king of spin, or, in the language of the day, the "prince of '''puff'''".}}
# {{lb|en|genetics}} A region of a [[chromosome]] exhibiting a local [[increase]] in [[diameter]].
# {{lb|en|slang|dated|UK}} [[life|Life]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1938|author=w:P. G. Wodehouse|title=The Code of the Woosters
|passage=Did you ever in your '''puff''' see such a perfect perisher?}}
# {{lb|en|derogatory|chiefly|northern England|slang}} {{syn of|en|poof|nodot=a}}: a [[male]] [[homosexual]], [[especially]] an [[effeminate]] [[one]].

