===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|puffen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|pyffan|t=to breathe out, blow with the mouth}}. Compare {{cog|nl|puffen}}, {{cog|nds-de|puffen}}, {{cog|de|puffen}}, {{cog|da|puffe}}, {{cog|sv|puffa}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To emit smoke, gas, etc., in puffs.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|month=January|author=David L. Smith|title=A Runaway at Beattock|journal=Railway Magazine|page=53|text=Still on the down line, the engine '''puffed''' away to the South, and in a few moments had disappeared in the darkness!}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[pant]].
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables|passage=By and by comes the ass back again, '''Puffing''' and Blowing, from the Chase.}}
#* {{RQ:Burroughs Land That Time Forgot||VI|passage='''Puffing''' and panting, we plodded on until within about a mile of the harbor we came upon a sight that brought us all up standing.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[advertise]].
# To blow as an expression of scorn.
#* {{RQ:South Twelve Sermons|passage=It is really to defy Heaven to '''puff''' at damnation.}}
# To swell with air; to be dilated or inflated.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1690|author=w:Robert Boyle|title=The Christian Virtuoso
|passage='tis easy for a man to have a great opinion of his own knowledge , and be '''puff'd''' up by it}}
# To breathe in a swelling, inflated, or pompous manner; hence, to assume importance.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1633|author=w:George Herbert|title=The Quip
|passage=Then came brave Glory '''puffing''' by.}}
# To drive with a puff, or with puffs.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Virgil|Georgic 1
|passage=The clearing north will '''puff''' the clouds away.}}
# To repel with words; to blow at contemptuously.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1685|author=w:John Dryden|title=The Twenty-Ninth Ode of the First Book of Horace
|passage=I '''puff''' the prostitute away.}}
# To cause to swell or dilate; to inflate.
#: {{ux|en|a bladder '''puffed''' with air}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|I|ii|passage=the sea '''puffed''' up with winds}}
# {{senseid|en|flatter}} To inflate with pride, flattery, self-esteem, etc.; often with ''up''.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1881|author=w:Benjamin Jowett|title=Thucydides Translated into English
|passage='''puffed''' up with military success}}
# To praise with exaggeration; to flatter; to call public attention to by praises; to praise unduly.
#* {{RQ:Macaulay Johnson|passage='''puffed''' with wonderful skill}}

